-- title:
--   Chevalley's lemma on ideals with zero intersection
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring in a fixed universe which is Noetherian, local with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m =$ `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A`, and $\mathfrak m$-adically complete in the sense of Mathlib's `IsAdicComplete` (that is, separated and complete for the $\mathfrak m$-adic filtration). Let $I : \mathbb{N} \to$ `Ideal A` be a family of ideals of $A$ indexed by the natural numbers which is antitone, so $I_m \supseteq I_n$ whenever $m \le n$, and assume that the infimum of the family in the lattice of ideals is trivial, $\bigcap_n I_n = \bot$. Then for every natural number $N$ there exists an index $n$ with $I_n \le \mathfrak m^N$. Thus the decreasing sequence $(I_n)$ with zero intersection is cofinal in the $\mathfrak m$-adic filtration: every $\mathfrak m$-adic neighbourhood of $0$ contains some $I_n$.
--
--   This is Chevalley's lemma, asserting that in a complete Noetherian local ring the $\mathfrak m$-adic topology is the finest topology for which a given decreasing sequence of ideals with zero intersection is a neighbourhood basis of $0$. It is used here to identify a kernel as the ideal generated by an explicit sequence, in [`PDivisibleGroup.ker_eq_span_range_nthSeries_of_comul_eq_adicEval`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.ker_eq_span_range_nthSeries_of_comul_eq_adicEval).
-- source:
